        "Name": "Elevated translocator protein in skull bone marrow is linked to human chronic pain",
        "ReferencesAndLinks": [
          "Mohammadian M, Efthimiou N, Gabriel K, Brusaferri L, Cooper-Hohn J, Kim M, Murphy JP, Alshelh Z, Grmek G, Schnieders JH, Chane CA, Carmichael TG, Yang D, Schubert JJ, Turkheimer FE, Cazuza RA, Grace PM, Catana C, Stufflebeam SM, Edwards RR, Napadow V, Sullivan K, Nahrendorf M, Price TJ, Gilman JM, Loggia ML. Evidence for a role of skull bone marrow in human chronic pain. Sci Transl Med. In press. doi: 10.1126/scitranslmed.aed8729"
        ]
      },
      "readme": "README\n======\n\nDataset title\n-------------\nElevated translocator protein in skull bone marrow is linked to human chronic pain\n\n\nDescription\n-----------\nThis dataset contains multimodal neuroimaging data acquired from patients with chronic low back pain,\npatients with knee osteoarthritis, and healthy controls. Data include positron emission tomography\n(PET) with the radiotracer [11C]PBR28 and structural MRI (T1-weighted).\n\n\nParticipants\n------------\nParticipants included patients with chronic low back pain, patients with knee osteoarthritis, and\nhealthy control subjects.\n\n\nImaging modalities\n------------------\n- PET: [11C]PBR28, a radiotracer that binds the 18 kDa translocator protein\n  (TSPO), a marker of neuroinflammation/immune cell activation. Acquired as\n  a 90-minute dynamic scan on a Siemens Biograph mMR PET/MR scanner; static\n  SUV images were reconstructed from the 60-90 minute post-injection window.\n\n- Structural MRI: T1-weighted MPRAGE, acquired on the same Siemens Biograph\n  mMR scanner at 3T, coregistered to the PET image.\n\n\nDe-identification\n-----------------\nAll structural MRI images (T1w) were defaced prior to public release to remove facial features and\nprotect participant privacy.\n\n\nStudy design\n------------\nThis is an observational, cross-sectional study. No experimental intervention or task was performed\nduring image acquisition.\n\n\nFunding\n-------\nThis work was supported by the National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (NINDS),\nNational Institutes of Health, under awards 1R01NS094306-01A1 and 1R01NS095937-01A1, and by the\nNational Institute on Drug Abuse (NIDA) under award 5R01DA053316-05.\n\n\nNotes\n-----\nThe dataset is organized according to the Brain Imaging Data Structure (BIDS) specification.\n",
